title: Amazing Grace
description: A short devotional on the hymn John Newton wrote in 1772, and what it says of God's grace to a wretch, then and even now.
output: lessons/history/amazing-grace.html

# From the notes (studies/data/notes/amazing-grace-notes.html), in the author's words, arranged on slides.

=== Amazing grace
--- Amazing Grace [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Amazing Grace
sub: John Newton, 1772
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- How sweet the sound
center: *Amazing grace! How sweet the sound,*
center: *That saved a wretch like me!*
center: *I once was lost, but now am found,*
center: *Was blind, but now I see*
note: John Newton, “Amazing Grace”

--- Truly a wretch
lead: That hymn, “Amazing Grace” (which is one of my favorites), was written by John Newton in 1772, just before 1776 and American Independence.
p: And as Pastor Tefft has often pointed out, Newton was truly a “wretch.”

--- A wayward sailor
p: Before his conversion to Christianity, John Newton was a wayward sailor, living a reckless and morally depraved life marked by profanity, drunkenness, debauchery, and involvement in the brutal Atlantic slave trade.
callout: And the song is about how amazing God’s grace is, “To save a wretch like me.”

--- True for all of us
lead: And that is true for all of us.
p: What an amazing, glorious and wonderful grace, that God abundantly gives, even when we were wretches. Even as sinners, Christ died for us. [[Romans 5:8]]

--- Even now
p: But when I sing Brother Newton’s inspired hymn, I often think about the grace God shows me even now.
p: What a wretch I continue to be, and yet God’s love is constantly shown to me. The Lord is still with me, and shall not forsake me. [[Hebrews 13:5]]
p: His grace for me is not only sufficient, but an amazement. That He loves a wretch like me, even me. [[2 Corinthians 12:9]]

